# Where TOUR.md is published, and which version of it is up there.
#
# WHY A STAMP RATHER THAN A FETCH
# The obvious check is to download the page and diff it. It cannot be done here.
# The artifact is PRIVATE: it is readable through the owner's claude.ai login,
# and `curl` gets the single-page-app shell or a Cloudflare 403. A CI runner has
# neither the login nor a browser, so a gate that fetched would fail on every
# run for a reason that has nothing to do with whether the page is current.
#
# Storing a claude.ai session token as a repository secret would make the fetch
# possible and was rejected: a personal credential with far more reach than this
# check needs, rotating on its own schedule, to verify a documentation page.
#
# WHAT THIS PROVES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
# `sha256` is TOUR.md as it stood when the page was last published. Part 4 of
# `compiler/bin/check-tour.sh` compares it against the file on disk, so EDITING
# TOUR.md WITHOUT REPUBLISHING TURNS CI RED. That is the whole staleness
# condition and it is caught with no network at all.
#
# It does not prove the page actually holds these bytes. Updating this line
# without republishing would satisfy the gate and mislead a reader, so the two
# belong in the same commit: republish, then stamp. The `verified` line records
# when the page was last read back and confirmed to match, which is the only
# evidence here that is not self-reported.

url       https://claude.ai/code/artifact/40f71cf4-d5d3-4b71-882c-ad73f630a03c
sha256    b00155e9797a2caaa940c464bb48b9a7c4f77999e1602d507145c6f545477fe3
verified  2026-08-21 (again, for F20) — chapter 5's list bullet called `[]` and
          `[x, ..rest]` "the two list patterns", which stopped being true when
          `[a, b]` became exactly-two; the map's list node said the same. Fetched
          before republishing and the page was still showing the old bullet —
          the stale state this stamp exists to catch, caught by it.

          Earlier the same day — republished, then fetched and read back against the file:
          heading, the mermaid map, and chapters 1–12 verbatim including
          chapter 10's binary segments, which did not exist when the page was
          first published.

          THE STAMP SAT UNCOMMITTED FOR TWO DAYS AND THE PAGE WENT STALE IN
          THEM, which is the gate's own case made by its absence. This commit
          was written on 2026-08-19 and left on a branch; TOUR.md took 23
          commits of changes meanwhile — F13's whole binary chapter among them —
          and a reader with only the link saw none of it. The first thing part 4
          did on being run was go red, correctly, against the stamp it shipped
          with.
